+## JSON Patch generation (RFC 6902)
+
+```ts
+import { createPatch, applyPatch, JsonDiffError } from "./src/index.ts";
+
+const from = { name: "api", ports: [80, 443], debug: true };
+const to = { name: "api", ports: [8080, 80, 443] };
+createPatch(from, to);
+// [{ op: "add", path: "/ports/0", value: 8080 }, { op: "remove", path: "/debug" }]
+applyPatch(from, createPatch(from, to)); // deep-equal to `to`
+```
+
+- `createPatch(from, to)` returns a JSON Patch that turns `from` into `to` when applied with
+  `applyPatch`. It never modifies `from` or `to`, and the patch shares no object with them.
+- The patch describes only what changed, with `add`, `remove` and `replace` operations on the smallest
+  locations that differ: equal documents (object members in any order) give `[]`; objects are compared
+  member by member; a value whose type changes (an array becoming an object, a string becoming a
+  number...) is replaced whole, and so is the document itself (path `""`) when its root type changes.
+- Arrays are aligned on their longest common subsequence: an element inserted or removed anywhere is one
+  `add` or `remove`, without resending the elements after it, and an element that changed in place is
+  diffed inside. Once their common start and end are set aside, arrays whose sizes multiply to more than
+  4 000 000 are compared position by position instead, to bound memory: the patch is still correct,
+  but may be larger.
+- `move` and `copy` are never generated.
+- It throws a `JsonDiffError` when `from` or `to` is not a JSON value, by the same rules as `applyPatch`.
+  Its `cause` is the underlying error, and no other error escapes, even one raised by a getter.
+
 Run the tests with `npm test` (Node.js 22.18 or later, which runs TypeScript directly).
